lab-process-mail hands every inbox message to an agent, and the list
takes posts from anyone who can reach lab-mail. This series makes
sure only mail from an approved sender drives a pass. Anything else
is quarantined before the agent starts.

- Patch 1 gives the quarantined tag to every message under the list's
  quarantine/ maildir, and keeps it out of the inbox. The tag comes
  from where the file lives, like every other derived tag, so moving
  the file back releases the message.
- Patch 2 checks the sender in the loop, not in the agent. The From
  must name exactly one address, and it must match a line of the
  senders file in full, ignoring case. A message that fails the check
  moves into quarantine/, and the move is committed and pushed on the
  outbox bookmark like a reply. The loop then goes on to the next
  message. Without a senders file the loop refuses to start.

The packaged senders file lists pat@patmaddox.com and pat@p7x.dev.
LAB_PROCESS_MAIL_SENDERS points it elsewhere.

This checks the From header and nothing else. It keeps a stray post
from driving the agent, but it does not stop a forged From. That
needs lab-mail or Fastmail to check the sender first.

Quarantine works on the jj maildir, since that is what
lab-process-mail reads today. Once it reads the list from lab-mail,
the move becomes an IMAP MOVE into the quarantine folder, and the
agent's login will need that right.

kyua test in src/lab-process-mail passes 18 of 18, with three new
cases for the senders file, quarantine, and case. I ran patch 1's hook
against a scratch notmuch database: a message under quarantine/ is
tagged quarantined and not inbox, and moving it back gives the inbox
tag again.

